  abstract = {This article presents an extension to classical planning techniques that facilitates their use in dynamic multi-agent domains. The approach augments an integrated model of plan generation, execution and repair with two additional capabilities. The first is a general approach to multi-agent reasoning that allows an agent to reason about the plans of other agents and alter its planning behavior based on its relationship to those agents. Second, it incorporates an approach to plan management that implements higher-level planning constructs such as intentions to structure multiagent interactions. Introduction Recent applications have illustrated the power of planning techniques in remarkably complex applications such as virtual-reality tutoring systems (Rickel and Johnson, 1997), controlling unmanned vehicles (Lee et al., 1994) and a variety military command and control problems (Hill, 1997; desJardins and Wolverton, 1998; Wilkins and Myers, 1996). Applications such as these require the i...},
